<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Battle of Bud Dajo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1906</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Moro Crater massacre, Bud Dajo massacre · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moro-rebellion">Moro Rebellion</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Battle of Bud Dajo, also called the Moro Crater massacre, was an American assault in March 1906 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tausug">Tausug</a> Muslims who had taken refuge in the crater of the volcano Bud Dajo on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a> in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Of some 800 to 1,000 people in the crater, including many women and children, only a handful survived. It was among the bloodiest episodes of the American conquest of the Philippines and drew criticism in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-bud-dajo.jpg" alt="First Battle of Bud Dajo" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pershing, John J. (John Joseph), 1860-1948. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bud_Dajo_Massacre_Trench_on_Jolo_Island_c1906.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
